From 73ea5e4cc9bc5f390ff65692616350e8158c5421 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christoph Engelbert (noctarius)" <chris@simplyblock.io>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 18:06:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nfsd: keep a SCSI layout's device ID stable across a
 filesystem grow

xfs_growfs increments the filesystem's generation, and nfsd puts that
generation into the device ID of every layout it hands out. A block layout
client needs it: its device address carries the device's size. A SCSI
device address does not. It is the device's designator and the client's
reservation key, and neither changes when the filesystem grows. GETDEVICEINFO
finds the device by its index alone, and LAYOUTCOMMIT does not compare the
device ID it is sent.

The new generation still makes every SCSI client treat the grown device as
a device it has not seen, and look it up again. The Linux client looks a
SCSI device up in the mount namespace of the task that asked for the
layout. When that is an application container whose /dev has no
disk/by-id, the lookup fails ("pNFS: no device found for volume"), the
client stops using layouts and writes through the metadata server, and the
writes it had in flight under the old layout reach neither. A pNFS metadata
server whose export was grown while clients wrote served 1.5 MiB that a
client had written as allocated, zeroed blocks.

A SCSI layout now carries generation 0. The block layout type keeps the
filesystem's generation.
---
 fs/nfsd/blocklayout.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/nfsd/blocklayout.c b/fs/nfsd/blocklayout.c
index cea27017d..fc1d83e70 100644
--- a/fs/nfsd/blocklayout.c
+++ b/fs/nfsd/blocklayout.c
@@ -98,6 +98,17 @@ nfsd4_block_proc_layoutget(struct svc_rqst *rqstp, struct inode *inode,
 		goto out_layoutunavailable;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The filesystem bumps its generation when it grows, so that block
+	 * layout clients refetch a device whose size changed. A SCSI device
+	 * address is the device's designator and the client's reservation key,
+	 * and neither changes with the size. A new generation only makes every
+	 * SCSI client look the device up again, which fails for a client whose
+	 * lookup runs where the device is not visible.
+	 */
+	if (args->lg_layout_type == LAYOUT_SCSI)
+		device_generation = 0;
+
 	error = nfsd4_set_deviceid(&bex->vol_id, fhp, device_generation);
 	if (error)
 		goto out_error;
